from common import *
it('KERNEL-0185','rule','3.4','The doors: insertChain is the only door for new edges in a plat (a transient arena opening builds its input rings as exact topology); removeEdges and clearFiner the only doors for removing edges; insertChain vertex rows (opts.knots) and splitThrough row the only doors for adding a vertex role row (with retypeEdges rekey, which moves rows between entities); removeEdges on a VertexRef the door for stripping one (an S8 portal whose lane is removed, §4.6; a tower wallToWay removes, §4.11); warp the only door for moving existing vertices (moveVertex edit = a warp whose field displaces one vertex, resolved by resolveVertexAt; smoothing committed geometry = a warp whose field the smoother computes from the chain current vertices). Every other writer is built from these, so planarity, minEdge, clearance and kept content are properties of this list rather than of every stage (§3.10).',['§3.4 doors paragraph','§3.1 curves row (warp)'],'kernel',['@insertChain','@removeEdges','@clearFiner','@splitThrough','@retypeEdges','@warp'],['@invariants','§4.6','§4.11','§7.1'],['the doors','only door'])
it('KERNEL-0186','rule','3.4','Each op has documented preconditions, work local to the touched faces, a typed result, a declared operation count and fuzz tests with constructive degeneracies (§13.1).',['§3.4 doors paragraph'],'kernel',[],['§13.1 fuzz tests'],['op contract'])
it('KERNEL-0187','rule','3.4','sideRuns gives stages a side notion surviving curved, round-joined kerbs (maximal runs within a cumulative turning tolerance, each with its chord direction), which the bent cut needs (§4.6).',['§3.4 sideRuns paragraph'],'kernel',[],['§4.6 bent cut'],['sideRuns side notion'])
it('KERNEL-0188','rule','3.4','setOwner and retypeEdges are the calls §6.3 succession uses: when a re-derivation hands a carried address to a successor, the same batch re-owns the successor faces and roles under that address, so geometry and address always move together.',['§3.4 succession paragraph'],'kernel (succession owned by §6.3)',['§6.3 succession'],['§6.3'],['succession'])
it('KERNEL-0189','rule','3.4','Profiled chains: a chain carrying a profile is inserted with its knots (opts.knots: chain index of each knot vertex with its knot role and ordinal); a knot that attraction lands on an existing vertex is carried to that vertex, so the profile keeps its knot. A chain cut into parts owned by different entities, a ring opened, or two chains joined go through retypeEdges rekey, which inserts and drops cut knots by §2.2 rule 5, renumbers each entity knots in chain order and returns the new profiles.',['§3.4 profile paragraph'],'kernel',['§2.2 rule 5','@attraction'],['@rekey','§7 way split/join edits'],['knot carry'])
it('KERNEL-0190','mechanism','3.4','The regular fit (item 45; J-A45.31): regularFit is the ONE computation of a ring best-fit regular polygon so castle repair (§4.8) and equalize warp brush (§7.1) cannot drift apart. Reads the ring counter-clockwise (clockwise in reverse) from its lowest-leftmost vertex p0 through p(n-1); centre c = ring area centroid summed from area2 shoelace terms on bounding-box-relative coordinates in that order, divided through ratio() (§3.2 (6), (8)); first Fourier component a = (1/n) * sum_k (p_k - c) * e^(-2 pi i k / n), each vertex a complex number, summed in ring order; returns radius |a|, phase arg a, and per vertex target t_k = c + a * e^(2 pi i k / n): the regular n-gon about c whose corners lie nearest the ring own in the least-squares sense, vertex k to the fit k-th corner. Targets do not depend on the start vertex (starting one later turns a by one step of 2 pi/n), so the fixed start fixes only summation order. The area centroid (not the vertex average) keeps a side cut into many short edges from pulling the centre.',['§3.4 The regular fit'],'kernel',['@area2','@ratio','§6.5 dmath'],['§4.8 castle repair','§7.1 equalize brush'],['regular fit algorithm','first Fourier component'])
it('KERNEL-0191','rule','3.4','regularFit numerics: cosines, sines and phase from dmath (§6.5 (2)), lengths from Math.sqrt (exact by specification), so every host returns the same doubles; compactness = 4 pi A / P^2 with A from area2 and P the summed side lengths. The service is O(n), reads no arena and writes nothing.',['§3.4 The regular fit'],'kernel',['§6.5 dmath','@area2'],['@RegularFit'],['regularFit determinism'])
it('KERNEL-0192','rule','3.4','regularFit callers move vertices toward targets only through warp (§3.6) - whose rounding, whole-step validation, halving and held vertices apply - or, for a ring not yet committed (a castle claim at S3), by building the moved ring and inserting it as any claim; a vertex the caller pins (§4.8 pinned vertices: an on_enceinte claim weld pair and, once the gate is fixed, the two corners of the claim side holding the town-side gate) gets no displacement, while every target is still computed from the whole ring. Degenerate input refused as the §3.1 row states. Fixture: §13.1.',['§3.4 The regular fit'],'kernel',['@warp','@insertChain'],['§4.8 castle','§13.1 regularFit fixture'],['regularFit callers','on_enceinte weld pair'])
it('KERNEL-0193','mechanism','3.4','Stretches (J-K31): every operator edit on part of a chain names the part by a StretchRef (§2.2 rule 5) and hands it to removeEdges or retypeEdges, so it passes through the listed doors and their invariant coverage. The op resolves the ref at the batch current state: each end must be a vertex of the chain (named by a vertex role or its lattice point) and both must lie on one part of it, else E_DEGENERATE detail stretch; an end finer than the chain is first promoted to the chain level (W_PROMOTED, listed in the commit record; its split flag cleared), because it becomes an end of the remaining parts.',['§3.4 Stretches','§3.1 E_DEGENERATE comment'],'kernel (StretchRef record owned by §2.2 rule 5)',['§2.2 rule 5 StretchRef'],['@removeEdges','@retypeEdges','@frameGrowth (b)','@clearRegion preparation','§7 operator edits','§13.1 fixtures'],['StretchRef','stretch','J-K31'])
it('KERNEL-0194','rule','3.4','Stretch removal on profiled chains: removeEdges gives each remaining part of a profiled chain a cut knot at the stretch end on its side (§2.2 rule 5) before the stretch rows and its interior knot rows leave, so every remaining sample is unchanged; retypeEdges rekey cuts at the two ends as at any cut vertex.',['§3.4 Stretches'],'kernel',['§2.2 rule 5'],['@sampleProfile'],['cut knot at stretch end'])
it('KERNEL-0195','rule','3.4','A removal that would leave a linear entity in more parts than it declares fails role consistency at commit (§3.10), so an operator cutting a way in two first hands the far part to a new entity with rekey on that part StretchRef. A cut end not yet a vertex is placed first by splitThrough with the vertex role row the operator wants on it (an anchor contact name, given without an ordinal, §3.3 rule 2). Fixtures: §13.1.',['§3.4 Stretches','§3.3 rule 2'],'kernel',['@splitThrough','@rekey'],['@invariants role consistency','§13.1'],['declared parts'])
it('KERNEL-0196','rule','3.4','Arenas, keys and services (J-A12.32): every writer acts on the arena its transaction key names; every service reading an arena takes it by key: resolve, pointLocate and faceNeighbours an ArenaKey (a PlatKey or a transient arena TransientKey); resolveVertexAt, signature and sampleProfile (which read a plat role rows) a PlatKey. The arenas share no topology (§2.2 rule 2), so an op handed a slot of another arena is a programming error.',['§3.4 Arenas, keys and services'],'kernel',['§2.2 rule 2','§2.2 ArenaKey/PlatKey'],['every service caller'],['ArenaKey','PlatKey','TransientKey','J-A12.32'])
it('KERNEL-0197','rule','3.4','Slot stamping: every slot a caller holds comes from a result stamped with its ArenaKey and arena version (Resolved, a query result, a commit record, ChainInsert.placed), valid until that arena next commit (§2.2 rule 5); a debug build carries each slot as a handle stamped with both and asserts wherever passed that its key is the arena the call acts on (the transaction key for an op and for a service handed a slot alone - kerb, offsetFace, insetRing, sideRuns, sampleBoundary, which read the open transaction arena and are called only inside one - the key argument otherwise) and that its version is current, as resolve already asserts. A cross-arena link is an entity id, never a slot.',['§3.4 Arenas, keys and services'],'kernel',['@resolve'],['debug build assertions','§13.1'],['stamped slot','debug assertion'])
it('KERNEL-0198','rule','3.4','Cross-arena reads: a stage on one arena reads another through the services alone and never writes it: the underground stage tests what it lays against the ground by pointLocate and resolve on the ground PlatKey (what it tests and what excludes a sample are II.7.3 surface constraint, item 12); a sky island shadow footprint is the same query the other way.',['§3.4 Arenas, keys and services'],'kernel (constraints owned by II.7.3 / V04 underground, V05 sky)',['@pointLocate','@resolve'],['II.7.3','V04 underground','V05 sky'],['cross-arena read','surface constraint'])
add(I)
